WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:15.915
In video three, we calculated the expected value, the variance and the standard deviation of a discrete random variable. In this video, we get to know rules of calculation with which these key figures can, in some cases, be determined more quickly.

2
00:00:17.615 --> 00:00:25.293
Once again, we look at the example of rolling two dice. The random variable X gives the sum of the two numbers.

3
00:00:25.843 --> 00:00:35.633
From video three, we know its expected value, seven, its variance, thirty-five sixths, and its standard deviation, approximately two point four one five.

4
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:45.377
First, we look at a casino game in which the sum of the two dice is the basis for the gain, and we want to determine the expected value and the standard deviation of this gain.

5
00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:49.487
The learning goals of this video are:

6
00:00:49.937 --> 00:00:57.938
First: to determine the expected value and the variance of a linear transformation of a random variable using rules of calculation.

7
00:00:58.338 --> 00:01:03.312
Second: to calculate the expected value of a sum of two random variables.

8
00:01:03.712 --> 00:01:09.298
And third: to calculate the variance of a sum of two independent random variables.

9
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:20.083
We begin with the linear transformation of a random variable. To do so, let a and b be two fixed numbers and X a random variable.

10
00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:33.139
We multiply X by a and then add b. The result, a times X plus b, depends on chance through X. Therefore, a times X plus b is itself a random variable as well.

11
00:01:33.739 --> 00:01:38.726
The factor a stretches the values of X, the constant b shifts them.

12
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:50.569
As an example, we consider a game in a casino. The stake is a fixed fifteen euros. In the game, two dice are rolled, and the casino pays out twice the sum in euros.

13
00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:57.147
The sum is again our random variable X. So the payout is two times X euros.

14
00:01:57.697 --> 00:02:14.118
We are interested in the gain, that is, the payout minus the stake. The gain is also a random variable; we call it G. G is defined as two times the sum X minus the stake of fifteen. The values of G have the unit euro.

15
00:02:14.718 --> 00:02:22.903
So the gain is a linear transformation of X, with the factor a equal to two and the constant b equal to minus fifteen.

16
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:34.699
On the left, we see the table of the probability mass function of X from video two. We extend it by a column for the realisations g of the gain.

17
00:02:35.249 --> 00:02:55.835
In the first row, both dice show a one, and the sum is two. The gain is two euros times the sum of two, minus the stake of fifteen euros. That makes minus eleven euros. So the game leads to a considerable loss. In the second row, the sum is three, and we calculate the gain as two times three minus fifteen, so again a loss, of minus nine euros.

18
00:02:56.435 --> 00:03:02.671
This continues up to the sum of twelve. There we get a positive gain of nine euros.

19
00:03:03.271 --> 00:03:13.605
To summarise once more: negative gains are losses. For a sum up to seven, you lose money; from a sum of eight onwards, you make a profit from the game.

20
00:03:14.205 --> 00:03:42.325
Based on this table, we can calculate probabilities for the gain G. Each realisation of the gain belongs to exactly one realisation of the sum X. The largest loss, a G of minus eleven euros, for example, occurs exactly when the sum is two. Therefore, this outcome of the game has a probability of one over thirty-six. In the same way, every gain g has the same probability as the corresponding sum x.

21
00:03:44.230 --> 00:04:01.330
Now we show both probability mass functions as bar charts, one above the other: at the top, the probability mass function of the sum X; below, the probability mass function of the gain G. The x-axis of the lower chart shows the possible outcomes of the game in euros.

22
00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:10.546
The sum two corresponds to the gain minus eleven. The bar has the same height in both charts, one over thirty-six.

23
00:04:11.097 --> 00:04:18.111
The sum three corresponds to the gain minus nine, again with the same probability, two over thirty-six.

24
00:04:18.711 --> 00:04:34.268
Overall, the bar heights are the same in both charts. Only the realisations on the x-axis differ. We convert the values of the sum x to the scale of the lower chart using two times x minus fifteen.

25
00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:50.877
How high is the gain on average in the long run, over many games? We could calculate the expected value of G using the definition: each realisation of G is multiplied by its probability, and all products are added up.

26
00:04:51.477 --> 00:05:07.176
If the expected value of X has already been calculated, it is quicker. For a linear transformation, the following holds in general: we obtain the expected value of a times X plus b by multiplying the expected value of X by a and then adding b.

27
00:05:08.176 --> 00:05:19.178
In our example, a equals two and b equals minus fifteen. With the expected value of seven for the sum, we get two times seven minus fifteen, which is minus one.

28
00:05:19.778 --> 00:05:26.372
So on average, you lose one euro per game. In the long run, the casino wins.

29
00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:37.753
There is also a rule of calculation for the variance: the variance of a times X plus b is the square of a times the variance of X.

30
00:05:38.453 --> 00:05:49.555
The constant b does not appear on the right-hand side. It shifts all realisations and the expected value by the same amount. The distances to the expected value stay the same.

31
00:05:50.105 --> 00:06:00.769
The factor a, on the other hand, multiplies all distances to the expected value. Since the variance is based on squared distances, a enters squared.

32
00:06:01.369 --> 00:06:16.022
For our game, the variance of X is thirty-five sixths. The variance of the gain is therefore two squared, that is four, times thirty-five sixths. This gives seventy thirds, approximately twenty-three point three three three euros squared.

33
00:06:16.622 --> 00:06:34.193
As usual, we obtain the standard deviation as the square root of the variance: the square root of seventy thirds is four point eight three euros. Here we see once again that converting the variance into the standard deviation is necessary to obtain the right order of magnitude and unit.

34
00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:52.992
We add these key figures to the two bar charts. At the top, the expected value of X is at seven; at the bottom, the expected value of G is at minus one. So in the long run, taking part in the casino game costs us one euro on average.

35
00:06:53.542 --> 00:07:09.146
At the top, starting from the expected value, we mark off the standard deviation of two point four one five to both sides to mark a typical range of the realisations. This range extends from approximately four point five eight five to nine point four one five.

36
00:07:09.696 --> 00:07:18.697
At the bottom, the standard deviation is four point eight three. The range here extends from approximately minus five point eight three to three point eight three.

37
00:07:19.247 --> 00:07:34.887
The expected value and the range of spread are in the same place in both charts, but adapted to the respective scale. In our example, they were converted in exactly the same way as the realisations: times two, minus fifteen.

38
00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:54.975
We now come to further rules of calculation for the expected value and the variance, which concern the sum of two random variables. To do so, we leave the casino game and go one step back by tracing the sum back to random variables for the two individual dice.

39
00:07:55.525 --> 00:08:03.676
The random variable X one gives the number on the red die, the random variable X two the number on the green die.

40
00:08:04.226 --> 00:08:11.128
A single die has a probability mass function in which each number from one to six occurs with probability one sixth.

41
00:08:11.678 --> 00:08:22.795
We already calculated the expected value of a single die in video three: each number is weighted with one sixth, which gives three point five. This applies equally to X one and X two.

42
00:08:23.395 --> 00:08:30.555
For the variance of a single die, we determine the distance of each number to three point five, square it and weight it with one sixth.

43
00:08:31.105 --> 00:08:40.354
The sum gives thirty-five twelfths, approximately two point nine one seven. Naturally, the variance is also the same for both dice.

44
00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:50.548
The sum of the two dice can be expressed with the random variables for the two individual dice: X equals X one plus X two.

45
00:08:51.098 --> 00:08:58.337
For the expected value of a sum of two random variables, the following always holds: it is the sum of the two expected values.

46
00:08:59.337 --> 00:09:05.128
For the sum of the dice, we get three point five plus three point five, which is seven.

47
00:09:05.728 --> 00:09:12.718
This is exactly the expected value that we calculated in video three using the probability mass function of the sum.

48
00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:23.521
There is also a rule of calculation for the variance of a sum. Here we consider the situation in which the two random variables are independent.

49
00:09:24.071 --> 00:09:40.329
Put simply, this means: if the value of X one is known, the same probabilities still apply to X two as without this information. With dice, this is the case: if the red die shows a six, each number on the green die still has probability one sixth.

50
00:09:40.929 --> 00:09:47.412
For independent random variables, the variance of the sum equals the sum of the two variances.

51
00:09:48.412 --> 00:09:55.893
For the sum of the dice, we get thirty-five twelfths plus thirty-five twelfths, which is thirty-five sixths.

52
00:09:56.493 --> 00:10:03.782
This is the same result as in video three, where we calculated the variance of the sum using its probability mass function.

53
00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:17.664
Let us summarise: for a linear transformation a times X plus b, the expected value of X is converted in the same way as the realisations: multiplied by a, then b is added.

54
00:10:18.264 --> 00:10:27.494
The variance is multiplied by the square of a; the constant b plays no role for it. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance.

55
00:10:28.094 --> 00:10:38.870
The expected value of a sum of two random variables is the sum of their expected values. This rule always holds, whether the random variables are independent or not.

56
00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:48.584
The variance of a sum is the sum of the variances. This rule, however, only holds if the two random variables are independent.
